this is what many villages in sudan's  southeastern ghadari state look like  days of torrential rains and the  seasonal flooding of the rahad river has  submerged homes  many have collapsed leaving residents  with nothing  the water came and flooded us and took  everything we used to have many books  but all of them are gone we lost our  savings food and furniture we have  nothing but to thank god  more than 4 000 homes have been  destroyed in 23 villages near sudan's  border with ethiopia most have been cut  off for nearly three weeks  authorities in the western ghalabat  region have provided some assistance but  it's nowhere near enough and they have  broadened their appeal describing the  situation as catastrophic  the directorate of health in the state  of ghidaraf provided medicines and  blankets  also the state emergency committee  provided what assistance it could  but it's not sufficient compared to the  size of the disaster  we urgently appeal to all sudanese  people to help us as well as  international organizations  [Music]  several people died when the raha river  burst its banks and hundreds are  displaced these families have found  shelter in a school it's only temporary  and although it's dry it lacks basic  necessities  we are in a very difficult situation our  children can barely survive here we need  tents food and water we need medicine  our children are suffering as we don't  have  large swaths of agricultural land are  waterlogged and livestock have been  swept away raising concerns of an  outbreak of disease and an environmental  disaster the raha river floods every  year but people here say this is the  worst they've seen in three decades  heber morgan al jazeera
 

